        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> However, this does not seem to work, since `:Telescope` is not available after restarting neovim.\n\nThe are several problems that cause this:\n- The main one is that `vim.pack.add()` doesn't receive expected arguments. Its first argument should be a list of plugin specifications (strings or `:h vim.pack.Spec` tables), but instead there is two separate tables: one for `telescope-fzf-native.nvim` and one for `telescope.nvim`. This makes 'telescope.nvim' not install and not load.\n- There is no 'plenary.nvim' dependency (at least in this snippet).\n\nWith this in mind, the following snippet works:\n\n```lua\nnow_if_args(function()\n  -- Define hook to build telescope and its dependencies\n  local build = function(plugin_name)\n    local path = vim.fn.stdpath('data') .. '/site/pack/core/opt/' .. plugin_name\n    local res = vim.system({ 'make', '-C', path }, { text = true }):wait()\n    if res.code ~= 0 then vim.notify('Build failed: ' .. res.stderr, vim.log.levels.ERROR) end\n  end\n\n  Config.on_packchanged(\n    'telescope.nvim',\n    { 'install', 'update' },\n    function() build('telescope.nvim') end,\n    'Build telescope'\n  )\n  Config.on_packchanged(\n    'telescope-fzf-native.nvim',\n    { 'install', 'update' },\n    function() build('telescope-fzf-native.nvim') end,\n    'Build telescope-fzf-native'\n  )\n\n  vim.pack.add({\n    'https://github.com/nvim-lua/plenary.nvim',\n    'https://github.com/nvim-telescope/telescope-fzf-native.nvim',\n    'https://github.com/nvim-telescope/telescope.nvim',\n  })\nend)\n```\n\nHowever, there is more that can be done. The `Config.on_packchanged` callback can be invoked with plugin data to spare extra `path` computation:\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/init.lua b/init.lua\nindex 0cd1ce7..1cbe1be 100644\n--- a/init.lua\n+++ b/init.lua\n@@ -42,7 +42,7 @@ Config.on_packchanged = function(plugin_name, kinds, callback, desc)\n     local name, kind = ev.data.spec.name, ev.data.kind\n     if not (name == plugin_name and vim.tbl_contains(kinds, kind)) then return end\n     if not ev.data.active then vim.cmd.packadd(plugin_name) end\n-    callback()\n+    callback(ev.data)\n   end\n   Config.new_autocmd('PackChanged', '*', f, desc)\n end\n```\n\nI'll adjust MiniMax code to have this today. So with the following small diff in place, the code for running `make` in the plugin's root is fairly concise:\n\n```lua\nnow_if_args(function()\n  -- Run `make` asynchronously. This does not report error, though.\n  local make = function(plug_data) vim.system({ 'make' }, { cwd = plug_data.path }) end\n\n  Config.on_packchanged('telescope.nvim', { 'install', 'update' }, make, 'Build telescope')\n  Config.on_packchanged('telescope-fzf-native.nvim', { 'install', 'update' }, make, 'Build telescope-fzf-native')\n\n  vim.pack.add({\n    'https://github.com/nvim-lua/plenary.nvim',\n    'https://github.com/nvim-telescope/telescope-fzf-native.nvim',\n    'https://github.com/nvim-telescope/telescope.nvim',\n  })\nend)\n```\n\n---\n\n> I think this would cover most of the hooks for package management that we can find in `LazyVim`, namely, `cmd`, `event` and `build`. WDYT?\n\nThe `cmd` and `event` are not really for package management, but for lazy loading. The equivalent of `event` is [`Config.on_event`](https://github.com/nvim-mini/MiniMax/blob/3d53b2a723084480705bb985d119a6f636a70856/configs/nvim-0.12/init.lua#L81-L82) and there is deliberately no equivalent of `cmd` (I don't see a robust and concise way to have this).\n\nThe example of `build` is already present for `vim.pack` itself in `:h vim.pack-events`. It has an example of running `make`. I think this should be enough.",
